export const body = "<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<ul><li>Project overview</li><li>Overcoming the challenges</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Organic and mechanical</li><li>The enormous scope</li><li>The gap between juniors and seniors</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Redefining the quality bar</li><li>The development cycle</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>The artist</li><li>The producer</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>The four measures for consistent quality</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>The mentor program</li><li>The review routine</li><li>The benchmark sharing</li><li>The quality check process</li></ul>\n\n<ul><li>Their achievements</li><li>Closing words</li></ul>\n\n<p>Since quality consistency is an all-time necessary topic, we will bring you another story about it today. This time, we will learn about an IP taking place in a fictional universe where animals have evolved into autonomous machines. These machines are intricate and enormous, which means their creators have to be absolutely talented. So, let’s see how these talented artists breathed life into them.</p>\n\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/269b2d24b3239870e047.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/269b2d24b3239870e047.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"767\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Project overview</h3>\n\n<p>Compared to the project, this team had a better starting point for this project since it is the sequel to a game we worked on years ago. The artist, the team lead, had worked on robots in the first game, and the AD has plenty of experience working with this client (he did environmental assets for the first game and cooperated with them on another IP). However, the rest of team were unfamiliar with the project.</p>\n\n<p>In the beginning, the client assigned us four robots with 400 to 500 man-days each. (Smaller ones were only 200 to 250 man-days.) But due to the pandemic, they had to retrieve several robots—some unfinished, some untouched—from other vendors. Eventually, they decided to send us one of those (which was a giant turtle). In total, we were responsible for nine different robots in this upcoming title.</p>\n\n<p>As of writing this article, they are working on the DLCs and expecting us to collaborate further.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/19dbbe22c40b06d9a25d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/19dbbe22c40b06d9a25d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"564\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Overcoming the challenges</h3>\n\n<p>There were three major obstacles for the team to overcome to maintain quality consistency:</p>\n\n<h4>Organic and mechanical</h4>\n\n<p>The project has a unique blend of organic and mechanical elements. Normally, the artists use Maya and 3ds Max for hard-surface objects while using ZBrush for rough-surface objects such as rocks, wood, clay, etc. This project, however, requires the team to use ZBrush to sculpt smooth hard-surface objects.<strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>For example, there was a robotic alligator whose task was cleaning the environment. To bring this animal to life, the artists had to maintain the structure of an alligator (so it can behave like one) while polishing the surface details as if they were polishing a car.</p>\n\n<p>Side note: because there is no rigger in the studio, the team found support from a rigging artist.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8e904b6366fef472b1a7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8e904b6366fef472b1a7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"222\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h4>The enormous scope</h4>\n\n<p>Those creatures were not only intricate but also huge, so team had to break each of them into smaller parts. That process allowed them to:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Share one asset among many artists</li><li>Create detailed briefs with step-by-step solutions</li><li>Do a lot of sharing about ZBrush &amp; Maya skills for this project (in addition to general training)</li></ul>\n\n<p>The team went as far as fine-tuning the ZBrush techniques for each artist. They acknowledged that different people have different wrist strength: their strokes will vary, the way they apply pressure will vary, so on and so forth. The team lead observed his team members and gave them personalized ZBrush coaching—brush configurations and usage, for example. There was also a standard pipeline for this project including how to use the brush and how to paint the shadows among other things.</p>\n\n\n<p>Figure 1 Optimus Primal Concept Art for The Last Knight - Transformers News - TFW2005</p>\n\n<h4>The gap between juniors and seniors</h4>\n\n<p>Adding new members to the team was difficult because of the steep learning curve. Their initial efficiency was 150%.</p>\n\n<p>Luckily, during the development of the first game, the studio had compiled and documented a step-by-step best practice about high-poly creation for this title. The team lead and one senior artist prepared those documents and shared their experience from the first game to everyone; they also had training for sculpting skills. Every new artist had to finish a training based on those documents (which lasted from 1 to 3 weeks). Only after passing the training, are the artists qualified for production.</p>\n\n<p>This training reduced the ramp-up time for the team and simultaneously gave them a chance to update the old training with new findings from the second game. They analyzed the feedback for this second game and condensed their knowledge to construct their own library on Confluence. Once the quality became consistent, the artists have been updating those documents according to their skills and understandings. (Their Confluence space is open to the whole team.)</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/99326c8c577e5c5a6380.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/99326c8c577e5c5a6380.webp\" width=\"1067\" height=\"439\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Redefining the quality bar</h3>\n\n<p>After identifying the obstacles, the next logical step was to set the quality bar to come up with appropriate measures.</p>\n\n<p>Since some of the team had already worked on the first game, the initial benchmark was based on its assets. However, there was a leap in quality in this sequel because it is a next-gen game. The client wanted more high poly details and a new approach to LOD control, so it was necessary redefine the bar.</p>\n\n<p>For this project, we received the concepts in ZBrush. During this state, they are rough and require manual cleanup. The team particularly enjoyed this process because it allowed them to be more creative than usual. They could add minor design elements to the concepts as long as they fit. Then, as they received more feedback from the client, they steadily raised the benchmark. Moreover, the artists never stopped improving the quality of their products on a daily basis. They truly embodied the saying: \"Practice makes progress\".</p>\n\n\n<h3>The development cycle</h3>\n\n<h4>The artist</h4>\n\n<p>After receiving the road map of the project from the client, the team immediately knew that those robots only had milestones instead of specific deadlines. Therefore, they were able to carry out a more efficient preparation phase.</p>\n\n<p>Initially, the client wanted them to assign 5 to 8 artists on this project. However, due to the new technical requirements, they offer a different approach to them:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Starting with 3 core artists</li><li>After the original 3 had mastered the craft, 3 new artists would be added</li><li>The new artists will then learn from these experienced artist</li></ul>\n\n<p>The average number of artists working on this project was 9, and the peak was 13. As for the technical requirements, the client specifically asked the team to test two different LOD pipelines, one which was approved (but reversed afterwards). The team paid close attention during this back-and-forth process and offered useful suggestions that helped finalize the LOD pipeline.<strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>The team reached 13 people because of an unexpected request from the client. They were so satisfied with the robot deliveries that they wanted us to work on some weapons too. Thanks to the well-established workflow, the team once again succeeded with flying colors. This, along with the additional assets from other vendors, changed our mandate scope, but our client was reasonable and willing to pay more for any testing work and design changes.</p>\n\n<p>Speaking of the client, they truly enjoyed working with the team—partly because of their performance, partly because of their approach to communication. There were daily conversations on Skype between two sides and biweekly meetings to update the progress. During these meetings, there were times when the team reminded the client of some detailed messages in their Skype communication. In short, this collaboration was mutually supportive and respectful.</p>\n\n<h4>The producer</h4>\n\n<p>While producers may not directly influence the creative process of artists, they are the conduit through which their team and client exchange information. The artist, the AP of this project, believes that<strong> clear</strong>,<strong> fast</strong>, and <strong>well-organized communication</strong> is of the utmost importance when it comes to project management.</p>\n\n<p>These are some tips from her:1</p>\n\n<p>Strive for transparent communication and keep everyone on the same page.</p>\n\n<p>For example, she wrote meeting notes for the kick-off meeting and biweekly sync-ups and shared them with the client as well as the stakeholders (PD, BD, GM, etc.).2</p>\n\n<p>Read brief documents thoroughly to have firm grasp on the client's expectations.</p>\n\n<p>This step helps bridge the information gap between artists and the client, getting rid of redundant questions.3</p>\n\n<p>Inform the client of the team's needs clearly.</p>\n\n<p>For example, when the team submitted the hi-poly stage for an entire robot, instead of waiting for the full review, they sent a checklist so that the client could approved individual parts. This approach allowed the team to continually make progress on a robot while waiting for feedback.4</p>\n\n<p>Prepare for biweekly calls and do not hesitate to raise a question.</p>\n\n<p>Before a call, she talks to the TL and writes down all the topics they are about to discuss, including weekly delivery plans, holiday heads-up, and other asset-specific questions. As for production questions, she will post them on Hansoft first to keep everything in a thread and remind the client to check it via Skype later.</p>\n\n<h3>The four measures for consistent quality</h3>\n\n<h4>The mentor program</h4>\n\n<p>The first layer of quality control is, what the team calls, the mentor program. Since the team consists of both old and new blood, they had to work in pairs—one senior overseeing one junior. The senior artist, the mentor, must have <strong>consistent quality</strong>, receive <strong>minimal feedback</strong>, be able to<strong> generalize his experience</strong> to the apprentice, and <strong>communicate effectively</strong>. This pairing method creates an opportunity for artists of different levels to hone their techniques, their artistic sensibility, and their communication skills.</p>\n\n<p>In the beginning, the managers (AD, TL, and APs) spent more than 50% of their time on assisting the artists and building their abilities, ownership, and good habits for this project. Once the team got up to speed, they were able to make progress themselves, resulting in improved quality and efficiency.</p>\n\n<h4>The review routine</h4>\n\n<p>On Mondays, the team hosted weekly meetings to determine their delivery milestones for each robot; on other weekdays, the team lead organized stand-up meetings (the goal is to let everyone do this in the future); in case of critical feedback, the whole team would have additional sync-ups.</p>\n\n<p>In terms of time management, they changed the planning unit from the traditional day-count to hour-count.<strong> </strong>This approach established a new habit for our artists: they had to make every work-hour count, instead of fluctuating their productivity and doing overtime to compensate.<strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<h4>The benchmark sharing</h4>\n\n<p>For junior artists, having a firm grasp on the quality bar is crucial. It means they know what to shoot for, and the higher-ups can spend less time on reviewing. So, the team leads created benchmark assets as references for their rookies to follow suit.<strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<h4>The quality check process</h4>\n\n<p>Due to the humongous scope of the project (and of each asset), the quality check process has three different levels:1The seniors and their paired artists are <strong>responsible for basic quality</strong>2Team leads check and help <strong>improve on the lacking areas</strong>3T he AD will check the combined product to make sure it <strong>good enough to submit to the client</strong></p>\n\n<p>The artist compared this mentor program to football team management. The team had a position called \"Data Analysis Manager\"; this person was responsible for:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Tracking daily efficiency</li><li>Generating weekly performance analysis</li><li>Notifying any changes (ups and downs) in efficiency to the team</li><li>Identifying causes for these changes<br>- If it increases performance, share the secret to the team to follow<br>- If it decreases the performance, inform the mentors so they can support their apprentices</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2254183f3037653c73c6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2254183f3037653c73c6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"767\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h3>Their achievements</h3>\n\n<p>Unlike in the previous game in which we only worked on hi-res models, we basically offered end-to-end service to the client with this title. Our high-quality deliveries and effective communication convinced the client to retain the team for future works. Moreover, this success paved the way for us to be more deeply integrated in the development cycle. Specifically, for this project, we helped them build the LOD pipeline and refined some design elements on their rough concepts in ZBrush.</p>\n\n<p>Another noteworthy point is how having a good relationship with the client resulted in a more positive team atmosphere. That atmosphere was a strong boost to the artists' confidence and self-esteem, which in turn motivated them to raise the quality bar higher and higher.</p>\n\n<h3>Closing words</h3>\n\n<p>The four measures team carries out serve two purposes: <strong>to</strong> <strong>deliver good quality on time</strong> and <strong>to</strong> <strong>grow the team</strong>. They believe these are two solid objectives to aim for because they allow the team to mature in many different areas beyond art—production, management, and communication.  Now, the team is in an ever-growing development cycle and able to work autonomously.</p>\n\n<p>For now, we hope this story can provide you with some valuable lessons about artistic and non-artistic quality control.</p>\n\n<p>Until next time, happy creating!</p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/19dbbe22c40b06d9a25d.webp", "assets/library-inline/2254183f3037653c73c6.webp", "assets/library-inline/269b2d24b3239870e047.webp", "assets/library-inline/8e904b6366fef472b1a7.webp", "assets/library-inline/99326c8c577e5c5a6380.webp"];
